WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.409
CLARIDA: Well, good morning, everyone.

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:05.430
I'm Richard Clarida.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.080
And I'll be moderating this session with John
Williams.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.850
This meeting is part of the C. Peter McColough
Series on International Economics.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.750
And so we'll be pursuing the following format.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:29.230
We'll begin with about 25 minutes or so of
a Q&amp;A session up here.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:35.760
And then the remaining 30 minutes will be
question and answer time for the audience.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:42.170
Well, when I got the request a couple of weeks
ago to moderate this session I accepted immediately.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.480
I, myself, have been looking forward to it.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.399
Just a brief introduction of our speaker,
and then we'll go right into the Q&amp;A.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:56.559
John Williams is the president of the San
Francisco Federal Reserve District Bank, and

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:00.749
has been president since 2011.

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:06.180
Before that, he was the director of research
at the San Francisco Fed and really, under

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.030
his leadership, turned into one of the very
top research groups in the Fed system.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:19.070
He began his career years ago at the Federal
Reserve Board and was part of the group there

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:25.130
bringing the Fed's modeling efforts into the
21st century.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:31.179
And many of us now actually use the Fed models
because they're now made available to the

00:01:31.179 --> 00:01:32.179
public.

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:37.979
And John had a big hand in developing that,
has a Ph.D. in economics from Stanford and

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:42.780
studied also at LSE and UC Berkeley.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.420
So let's get right into the opening set of
questions before we turn it over to the audience.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:54.509
So, John, what is your outlook for the U.S.
economy-unemployment, inflation, and growth?

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:59.710
And if that materializes, how many rate hikes
do you think you would be recommending?

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:04.869
WILLIAMS: So I think 2016's going to look
a lot like 2015 in many ways.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:11.870
I expect GDP growth to be about 2 percent-real
GDP growth this year, like we had last year.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:17.130
I think it's basically the same set of issues,
strong domestic growth especially in the service

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.500
sector, some subtraction from growth because
of the strong dollar and weakness abroad.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.140
So basically this balancing of the domestic
economy kind of doing well, but being-facing

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.860
some headwinds from abroad.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.810
I expect to see pretty solid growth this year,
like we saw last year, over 2 million jobs

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:41.380
this year and I expect the unemployment rate
to continue to edge down from 5 percent today,

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.920
down a few more tenths.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.821
My own view is that we're basically at full
employment or very near it based on 5 percent

00:02:47.821 --> 00:02:48.821
employment rates.

00:02:48.821 --> 00:02:50.170
And I feel that's very good news.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:55.280
And with a strong economy and with the dollar
and oil prices and other influences basically

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.320
turning more neutral in terms of inflation,
I view inflation as moving back gradually

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.680
over the next year or two to our two percent
goal.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.150
Like you said, I started this job in March
of 2011.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.930
It's been a long slog, but actually I think,
you know, on our full-employment mandate we're

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:13.930
basically there.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.710
And on our inflation mandate, I do think that
we not only made good progress but I'm pretty

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.720
optimistic that we'll get to our goal in the
next year or two.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.750
CLARIDA: And then if that-if that outlook
materializes, what rate hikes would you be

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:28.750
recommending?

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.760
WILLIAMS: Oh, I didn't get away with skipping
the rate question?

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.760
CLARIDA: (Laughs.)

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.760
WILLIAMS: Oh, OK.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.910
So, you know, I think that our basic strategy
is the right one.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:39.880
And that's, you know, slowly or gradually
removing accommodation, meaning raising interest

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:45.260
rates over the next few years, and getting
our interest rates back to normal.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.020
Now, the exact timing of when we have the
next rate increase will depend on the data,

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.360
depend on how we're doing relative to our
goals.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.970
But when I look back at the March economic
projections that my colleagues and I put out

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:03.050
suggesting, you know, over the rest of the
year, say, two or maybe three rate increases

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:10.040
this year, maybe one or two more next year,
so three or four next year, I think that's

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.040
still about right.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.040
It will depend on the data.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.720
We're still getting another month's of data
before the next-for the June meeting.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.310
I want to analyze that and come to a conclusion.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.340
Obviously we'll be meeting numerous times
a year, get to keep doing that, and think

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:23.340
about it.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.450
CLARIDA: Thank you.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.650
One of the bright spots in the economy recently
has been the pickup in wages.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:33.970
Not only are wages rising, but labor share
of national income has been rising.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.520
So how does the Fed factor that into its reaction
function for the economy?

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.520
WILLIAMS: Sure.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.310
And, you know, the wages has actually been
the puzzling part of the story.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.860
You know, wage growth has not picked-we kind
of would expect with unemployment coming down

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.650
to about 5 percent, wage growth would have
started picking up earlier.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.600
I think a number of factors have held wages
down.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.020
One is the very slow productivity growth and
some other factors.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.780
But we are seeing some signs-encouraging signs
that wages are starting to pick up.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.170
I think, you know, wages are the biggest cost
for businesses.

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:11.389
So that's going to put somewhat upward pressure
on inflation.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:15.360
I think we have to be realistic, kind of what's
the normal for wage growth.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.080
My own view is productivity growth probably
will average about 1 1/2 percent over the

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.080
next several years.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.280
So, you know, we might be seeing wage growth
of something like 3, 3 1/2 percent of normal.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.381
So we're moving towards that.

00:05:27.381 --> 00:05:29.220
And I think that's a positive sign.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.360
It shows, I think, a couple things: One is,
you know, that we are close to or at full

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.650
employment, with wage growth picking up that's
kind of a sign of where the labor market is.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:43.620
Obviously, it also is a sign that, you know,
we're seeing some uplift to inflation, which

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.840
is a good thing.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.270
CLARIDA: It is.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:52.500
Now, speaking of inflation, since the Fed
announced in January of 2012 that it was pursuing

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:58.550
a formal inflation target, inflation has undershot
that target for four years and will likely

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.650
to undershoot this year.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:06.170
So how would the Fed react to a modest overshoot
of inflation relative to target.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.040
Core CPI, for example, is already above 2
(percent).

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.040
Would it welcome that?

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.360
WILLIAMS: Yeah, this is probably-when you
say, how will the Fed react-is probably the

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.580
time when I should put in my normal disclaimer
that my remarks reflect my own views and not

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.580
necessarily anyone else.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:19.580
OK.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:20.580
CLARIDA: Sure.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.890
WILLIAMS: So you know, I think that-you know,
you're right.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.800
This has been an undershoot for several years.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:29.380
There is a-there were some signs that inflation
expectations have come down a bit.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:34.370
And that's somewhat worrying to me, because,
you know, having well-anchored inflation expectations,

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:39.050
people having confidence that the Fed will
achieve its goals is very important.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:43.639
So my view-my own forecast, my own view of
appropriate policy is we'll see inflation

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:48.370
move back to 2 percent, not overshoot by much,
by definitely get to 2 percent.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:54.400
If it overshot 2 (percent) by a few tenths
for a while, that wouldn't be a big problem

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.460
I think, you know, because, you know, we want
inflation to be an average, 2 percent, sometimes

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.700
a little bit above sometimes a little bit
below.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.889
I wouldn't want us to purposefully overshoot
inflation-the 2 percent goal by a lot and

00:07:04.889 --> 00:07:09.460
for an extended period, and here's why.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:14.231
If we overshoot inflation, inflation moved
to 2 1/2 or 3 percent, say, well, then we

00:07:14.231 --> 00:07:17.650
would have to bring inflation back down and
we would have to tighten policy and slow the

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:18.889
economy a lot.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:20.370
So I think that there are some risks.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:25.680
There are always risks to overshooting your
target and risks to, you know, having to undo

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:26.680
that.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.520
So, you know, my, you know, kind of perfect
landing for the economy would really have

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.560
inflation moving, you know, basically back
to 2 percent.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.380
And then from that point, you know, it sometimes
would be a little above, sometimes a little

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.970
bit below, but averaging 2 (percent).

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:39.970
CLARIDA: Mmm hmm.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:45.090
Now, on that point, we were chatting before
onstage that you attended a conference honoring

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.410
Mike Woodford, one of the, you know, great
thinkers of monetary economics.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:53.820
And of course, Mike's theoretical work has
pointed to the benefits of a version of inflation

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.940
targeting, price level targeting, which case
if the Fed were price level targeting it would

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.250
be aiming to overshoot now.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.250
WILLIAMS: Right.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.800
CLARIDA: The Fed's thinking on price level
targeting?

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.590
WILLIAMS: So, this is, I think, a pretty important
issue not just for the U.S. by for other countries.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:11.630
I mean, the U.S. and most-as you know-most
advanced economies, and many emerging market

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:17.450
economies and central banks have decided to
go some kind of version of inflation targeting,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:21.030
where you basically say you want inflation
to be 2 percent and if you're above you try

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.199
to bring it down to 2 (percent).

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:24.449
If you're below, try to move it back to 2
(percent).

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:29.330
And that's worked amazingly well across dozens
of countries over the last several decades.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.230
So it's been a big success.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:35.119
Price level targeting basically says, you
know, I want the price level to grow, say

00:08:35.119 --> 00:08:41.509
at 2 percent, but any undershoots or overshoots
are made up later.

00:08:41.509 --> 00:08:45.089
So you have this built-in memory that says
if you had low inflation for a few years,

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:49.070
you're going to have high inflation for the
next few years to compensation for that.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.820
Now, that's not the strategy we're doing.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.040
We're doing inflation targeting.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:56.459
I think it's really important for the Fed,
where we are, close to our goals, is to stay-not

00:08:56.459 --> 00:09:02.189
change, you know, horses in the middle of
the river, get our 2 percent goal, reach that

00:09:02.189 --> 00:09:04.199
and kind of focus on that.

00:09:04.199 --> 00:09:09.379
I do think that based on the work that Mike
Woodford and many others have done, and given

00:09:09.379 --> 00:09:14.699
the challenges that we've seen central banks
facing around the globe with achieving 2 percent-I

00:09:14.699 --> 00:09:20.540
mean, basically very few advanced economies
today have reached their goals; many have

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:27.619
been below for several years-that economists
and policymakers around the world, we think

00:09:27.619 --> 00:09:31.670
the strategy of inflation targeting, and think
hard about whether they should-we should be

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:33.699
doing some version of price level targeting.

00:09:33.699 --> 00:09:40.110
I think there is advantages in terms of situations
like the one we've been in the last few years.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:44.350
But I think that that's kind of a research
project that should be done for several years.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:48.160
And right now, in my view, is we should finish
that job that we set out.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.970
But I do think it's an important issue for
economists to be considering.

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:54.160
CLARIDA: OK.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:01.980
I think we've all noticed, those of us who
follow the Fed, that this Fed has been emphasizing

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:05.559
global developments as much or more than other
Feds.

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:09.380
And personally, I applaud the Fed for that.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:14.040
But does it complicate communications, since
the Fed's mandate is U.S. inflation and unemployment.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:18.230
Does it complicate communication to be discussing
global developments?

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:24.019
WILLIAMS: So as I was telling Rich this morning,
the answer is, yes, it does complicate our

00:10:24.019 --> 00:10:25.019
communications.

00:10:25.019 --> 00:10:26.019
And for the very reason you said.

00:10:26.019 --> 00:10:30.759
You know, our goals are domestic inflation,
domestic employment.

00:10:30.759 --> 00:10:35.720
That's what-everything that we talk about
at FOMC meetings when we think about the economy

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.889
and we do our analysis is focused through
the lens of, well, what does that mean for

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:44.279
employment in the U.S., what's it mean for
U.S. inflation, what's it mean for the outlook.

00:10:44.279 --> 00:10:49.249
But in the current situation in the last couple
years, several years in fact, global developments

00:10:49.249 --> 00:10:54.029
have been a big driver of what's happened
with U.S. inflation and with U.S. growth.

00:10:54.029 --> 00:10:57.179
I mentioned already the strong dollar and
the decline in oil prices.

00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:02.660
Well, those have had, you know, enormous impacts
on the U.S. economy and have been important

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:07.199
drivers of U.S. outlook and also risks to
that outlook.

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:11.709
So at the Fed and the FOMC we've been kind
of facing this challenge of how to communicate

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:13.170
that.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:17.709
We highlight the fact that our goals are domestic,
but these global developments are really important

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:21.360
to understand the outlook for the U.S. economy
and the risks to it.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.470
So I have been a supporter of putting in more
language in our statements and our descriptions

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:31.999
of our policy decisions that reflect global
developments, but it has led to some of these

00:11:31.999 --> 00:11:37.259
kind of questions that you brought up, is
are we trying to be the central banker for

00:11:37.259 --> 00:11:43.040
the world, are we taking into consideration
what's happening abroad in terms of our decisions.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.199
So it is a nuance issue, but I'm a big believer
in transparency.

00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:52.079
If our decisions to, you know, hold off on
raising rates in the past were influenced

00:11:52.079 --> 00:11:56.540
because of global developments and how they
impact the U.S. economy, I just personally

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:01.730
think we should explain that as best we can
just keep working on being better at explaining

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:02.730
our logic.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:07.800
CLARIDA: And just parenthetically, one thing
I think has been useful with the Fed providing

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:14.130
now-essentially the code to run its model
that John and others developed, you know,

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:17.629
many of us have been able to run simulations
on the Fed model for the effect of things

00:12:17.629 --> 00:12:19.639
like a 10 percent move in the dollar, or whatever.

00:12:19.639 --> 00:12:23.480
So you can actually see essentially the same
analysis that you're looking at as well, which

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.449
is that it does have an impact on both inflation
and employment.

00:12:27.449 --> 00:12:33.399
There are those who claim that there is a
risk to the Fed in reacting to the dollar,

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:40.760
for example, or global developments in markets,
perhaps overreacting to financial asset prices,

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.050
which is a general issue in monetary policy.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:44.792
What do you think about that?

00:12:44.792 --> 00:12:45.792
WILLIAMS: Right.

00:12:45.792 --> 00:12:46.792
We'll you've written about this yourself.

00:12:46.792 --> 00:12:47.792
(Laughs.)

00:12:47.792 --> 00:12:50.930
This is an old issue about how do the-you
know, the Fed obviously or any central bank,

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:56.069
but the Fed clearly has a huge impact on financial
market conditions, not only in the U.S. but

00:12:56.069 --> 00:12:57.069
globally.

00:12:57.069 --> 00:13:00.699
Obviously, financial market conditions then
impact the U.S. economy.

00:13:00.699 --> 00:13:04.470
And you know, sometimes when you're a policymaker
at the Fed you kind of feel like you're living

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.629
in a hall of mirrors where, you know, you
can't-you know, how do you-you know, if we

00:13:07.629 --> 00:13:11.230
do this then that will happen, then that would
mean we shouldn't do this.

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.100
So this is why we have economists.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:19.029
This is why we have models, is to try to think
about what are the-you know, kind of the endogenous

00:13:19.029 --> 00:13:24.970
or reactions in the economy to changing economic
conditions and the outlook versus some kind

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:30.639
of shock or some outside event that then feeds
back, and do our best to analyze, you know,

00:13:30.639 --> 00:13:34.559
what are the financial conditions, how they
feed that in-how does that feed into the U.S.

00:13:34.559 --> 00:13:37.009
outlook, and then what's the appropriate policy.

00:13:37.009 --> 00:13:41.860
You know, that's just part of the world that
we have to operate in.

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:46.929
And you know, I think it's really important
not to overreact to changes in financial conditions.

00:13:46.929 --> 00:13:50.059
I always tell people that I don't have a Bloomberg
terminal.

00:13:50.059 --> 00:13:52.739
I don't watch Kramer on CNBC.

00:13:52.739 --> 00:13:53.739
(Laughter.)

00:13:53.739 --> 00:13:57.369
I seriously do not-you know, as a policymaker,
you want to-you know, monetary policy as Milton

00:13:57.369 --> 00:14:01.309
Friedman taught us and has been studied for
decades, monetary policy takes a year or two

00:14:01.309 --> 00:14:03.939
to have its full effect on the U.S. economy.

00:14:03.939 --> 00:14:08.329
We've always got to be looking down the road
and thinking about what's the right decision.

00:14:08.329 --> 00:14:13.839
And I really do try to avoid kind of getting
caught up in the day-to-day fluctuations in

00:14:13.839 --> 00:14:14.839
markets.

00:14:14.839 --> 00:14:17.769
At the same time, you know, being aware of
what's happening in the world.

00:14:17.769 --> 00:14:21.279
So it is a challenge, but we try to do the
best we can.

00:14:21.279 --> 00:14:22.910
CLARIDA: Yeah.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:27.920
In addition to your full-time job as the president
of a district bank, you're also continuing

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:33.600
to do research, and in particular research
with Thomas Laubach at the board has been

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.149
quite influential, certainly on me and a lot
of others for thinking about this idea of

00:14:38.149 --> 00:14:42.429
the time variation and the equilibrium or
neutral real interest rate.

00:14:42.429 --> 00:14:47.589
So perhaps you could tell us the current state
of that work and, in particular, as I understand

00:14:47.589 --> 00:14:50.740
it, you're extending this to some other major
economies as well.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:51.740
WILLIAMS: Right.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:56.329
I mean, this is actually one of those times
where being a researcher at the Fed was a

00:14:56.329 --> 00:15:00.480
great opportunity for actually thinking about
issues that other people weren't.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.179
So back in the '90s-late '90s Thomas Laubach
and I were starting to think about, you know,

00:15:05.179 --> 00:15:07.649
what's the normal or neutral interest rate.

00:15:07.649 --> 00:15:12.569
And it all started with this idea of a Taylor
rule, which is some kind of mathematical equation

00:15:12.569 --> 00:15:17.170
that tells, you know, a central bank how to
move interest rates as the economy changes.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:21.179
And that has this-you know, the Taylor rule
has a notion of a normal or neutral or equilibrium

00:15:21.179 --> 00:15:22.179
interest rate.

00:15:22.179 --> 00:15:26.179
And so my colleague, Thomas Laubach, and I
have developed a model on that.

00:15:26.179 --> 00:15:31.100
And one of the things that, you know, in neutral
interest rates in the past, which tended to

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:35.589
be between 2 percent or 3 percent on a real
basis, inflation-adjusted basis.

00:15:35.589 --> 00:15:43.231
So a short-term interest rate of, say, 4 or
5 percent would be normal in the past, in

00:15:43.231 --> 00:15:44.231
our estimates.

00:15:44.231 --> 00:15:50.640
Then we saw a dramatic change over the last
few decades, but especially since 2007, where

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:56.339
our analysis says that at least currently
the U.S. economy, the neutral interest rate-again,

00:15:56.339 --> 00:16:02.449
on a real basis, an inflation-adjusted basis-is
about zero percent, which means that, you

00:16:02.449 --> 00:16:06.279
know, a 2 percent short-term interest rate
is a neutral interest rate.

00:16:06.279 --> 00:16:08.029
This is a huge decline.

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:10.160
It reflects slower trend growth in the economy.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:16.910
It reflects other global factors that are
pushing down globally kind of neutral interest

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:17.910
rates.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.509
And there's been a lot of research on this,
a lot of analysis.

00:16:19.509 --> 00:16:24.629
But one of the things that we've been working
on now is looking is this just in the U.S.,

00:16:24.629 --> 00:16:26.449
is this something particular to the U.S.?

00:16:26.449 --> 00:16:31.239
I mean, often Americans and American economists,
because we have all this data, we tend to

00:16:31.239 --> 00:16:36.410
focus on our country and kind of ignore other
countries too much.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:41.910
So we've been looking at Europe, Canada, and
Britain as using our model.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.480
And what we're seeing is a very similar pattern
in all of those countries.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.980
A couple things: One is slower trend growth.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:54.319
Basically because of demographics in all these
countries is slower population in labor force

00:16:54.319 --> 00:16:57.449
growth, but also slower productivity growth.

00:16:57.449 --> 00:16:59.859
Similar patterns across all the other advanced
economies.

00:16:59.859 --> 00:17:03.509
We're looking at, and in each case, we're
finding this neutral interest rate coming

00:17:03.509 --> 00:17:08.819
down significant, and especially in the last
decade, to numbers that were much lower than

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:09.929
we've ever found.

00:17:09.929 --> 00:17:15.000
I think this is a-you know, we can-there's
a debate among economists about, you know,

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.290
will this revert back to normal eventually?

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:22.059
But it does present a significant challenge
to central banks, because if you're neutral

00:17:22.059 --> 00:17:27.299
interest, you know, your normal interest rate
is, say, 2 or 3 percent, that means when the

00:17:27.299 --> 00:17:31.440
next recession happens, you know, once we
get out of this-you know, for those countries,

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:35.600
once they get out of it, they're going to
be starting with an interest rate that's like,

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:37.280
you know, 2 or 3 percent.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:41.860
Typically, in the U.S., when there's a recession,
the Fed cuts interest rates by about 5 percentage

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:42.860
points.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:46.220
So you know, we hit the zero lower bound this
time in 2008.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:50.940
Basically, if this kind of research is right,
then countries in the future are going to

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:55.850
be hitting the zero lower bound and basically
finding monetary policy struggling and needing

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:58.510
to go to unconventional policies much more
frequently.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:03.370
I think it raises a whole bunch of issues
that we need to be thinking more about in

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:05.080
terms of the future.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:09.840
CLARIDA: John, were talking right before we
came in that one of the things that's noteworthy,

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:14.980
at least to me, about this-about this cycle,
of course the Fed hiked in December, but at

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:21.429
least, in my case, going back to the 1960s,
I think this'll be the first rate hike cycle

00:18:21.429 --> 00:18:25.620
for the Fed in which its actually beginning
to hike rates when inflation is below the

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.799
desired level.

00:18:26.799 --> 00:18:31.330
So typically a rate hike cycle occurs because
the Fed wants to reduce inflation or, as in

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:36.100
'94 and '04, it begins to hike when it wants
to keep inflation at the current level.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:40.960
So does this present a communications challenge,
hiking when you're also saying we want inflation

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:41.960
to go up?

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:45.630
WILLIAMS: Apparently it does-(laughter)-based
on my email that I get and the bloggers comments

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:49.290
about some of our-some of the things I've
said about the need to raise interest rates

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:51.970
even though inflation is below 2 percent.

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:56.690
So I think-first of all, I think it's actually
a good thing that we're doing this because

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:58.840
of this lag in monetary policy.

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:02.461
Again, it takes a-well, based on research,
it takes about 2 years for monetary policy

00:19:02.461 --> 00:19:04.340
to affect inflation rates.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:09.620
So we really need to be thinking where is
inflation going to be, not in, you know, mid-2016,

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:13.299
but where is it going to be in 2017, 2018?

00:19:13.299 --> 00:19:17.980
And you know, as you know, Rich, the history
of the Fed has not always been a good one.

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:22.740
In the past, there's been the stop and go
policies, especially back in the '60s and

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:28.830
'70s, where the Fed did wait around too long,
allowed inflation to get too high, then had

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:33.620
to put on the breaks, cause a recession, and
then take-you know, put the pedal to the metal

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:35.580
again to get the economy going.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:40.279
We're trying to avoid that on and off kind
of cycle, and just gradually move interest

00:19:40.279 --> 00:19:45.570
rates over the next several years back to
normal in anticipation of where the economy

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.260
is going to be in the next couple years.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:52.380
In terms of communication, it definitely does,
you know, raise the question that people say,

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:53.380
what's the rush?

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:58.289
You know, why are you raising rates when inflation
is still about 1 1/2 percent on kind of an

00:19:58.289 --> 00:19:59.289
underlying basis?

00:19:59.289 --> 00:20:02.850
You know, and my answer is: You know, we were
at zero for seven years.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:04.030
We've only raised the rate once.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:07.919
I don't know how the word "rush" fits into
that description of the Fed.

00:20:07.919 --> 00:20:08.919
CLARIDA: (Laughs.)

00:20:08.919 --> 00:20:15.019
WILLIAMS: But again, I think we're trying
very hard to balance the need to be forward

00:20:15.019 --> 00:20:20.610
looking, but also the fact is that this is
a global environment where growth is going

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:25.760
to be slow and there's a lot of disinflationary
pressures globally, even though the U.S. economy

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.080
is doing better.

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:33.220
CLARIDA: So the Fed, of course, has a large
balance sheet, about $4.5 trillion, reflecting

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.200
the quantitative easing programs.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:41.269
And at various times in minutes and statements,
the Fed has also-Fed officials have also indicated

00:20:41.269 --> 00:20:46.330
a desire to have the balance sheet shrink
over time.

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:51.090
So could you update us on the current state
of play of the Fed's thinking on this, because

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:52.280
it has evolved.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:58.789
And in particular, when the balance sheet
does begin to shrink, how will that occur?

00:20:58.789 --> 00:20:59.789
WILLIAMS: Right.

00:20:59.789 --> 00:21:04.660
Well, the balance sheet, you know, started
at about, you know, 850, 900 billion (dollars)

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:07.250
when we started in 2007.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:10.639
And as you said, we're over $4 trillion now.

00:21:10.639 --> 00:21:16.190
And most of that increase is due to the quantitative
easing programs that-QE programs that we've

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:22.169
done, which increased our asset side and obviously
equal increase in the liability side in terms

00:21:22.169 --> 00:21:24.559
of reserves and the Federal Reserve system.

00:21:24.559 --> 00:21:29.410
So the current thinking is that we're going
to use the Fed funds target, the short-term

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:34.400
interest rate, as our primary policy tool
to adjust to reflect macro circumstances,

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.240
and leave our balance sheet at least for the
time being where it is.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:38.500
And how do we do that?

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:44.120
Well, as our assets basically mature or are
paid off, we're reinvesting the proceeds from

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.740
that to keep the quantity of our balance sheet
at the same level.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:51.530
And we've been doing that for years now, and
we'll continue to do that for the time being,

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:57.380
and basically adjust the interest rate to
changing economic conditions and outlook.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:22:02.210
Then, once we get well-away from zero, basically.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.900
Once the normalization is-we've made enough
progress on that, then we're going to allow

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:11.290
the balance sheet-the plan is to allow the
balance sheet to basically organically decline

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:12.559
over time.

00:22:12.559 --> 00:22:17.830
And what I mean by organically is that, you
know, we hold Treasury securities, they mature

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:21.370
eventually, when they mature we just won't
reinvest that.

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:25.649
And our mortgage-backed security portfolio,
those mortgages do get paid off, you know,

00:22:25.649 --> 00:22:29.019
either through principal payments or people,
you know, paying off their mortgages.

00:22:29.019 --> 00:22:33.761
And over many years-well, actually, many,
many years, that side of the balance sheet

00:22:33.761 --> 00:22:34.761
will come down.

00:22:34.761 --> 00:22:39.950
Now, based on our own projections and, you
know, analysis we do, once we start this process

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:44.389
of allowing the balance sheet to shrink over
time, it would get back to a more normal level

00:22:44.389 --> 00:22:47.100
in, you know, about, you know, six or more
years or something.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:53.140
So it's going to be a very gradual process
of moving that back to whatever the new normal

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:54.140
there is.

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:58.799
Now, I think the point is really about communications
in my view, is that the Fed funds target is

00:22:58.799 --> 00:23:04.059
really the active tool that we're using, either
up or down, to respond to changing circumstances.

00:23:04.059 --> 00:23:07.450
And the balance sheet is basically just happening
in the background.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:12.990
Now, the good news for transparency is every
Treasury security, all the securities we owe,

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:17.450
is publicly available on the Fed-New York
Fed's website.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:19.659
So you know, people can plan ahead.

00:23:19.659 --> 00:23:22.020
And I don't think there'll be disruption when
we start that.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:26.250
So it's really just a matter of having that
happen slowly over time.

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:30.049
Now, the big question, I will tell you, is
what's going to be the new normal for the

00:23:30.049 --> 00:23:31.049
balance sheet?

00:23:31.049 --> 00:23:34.490
And that's something that we haven't actually
decided.

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.820
This is something we are studying.

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:41.440
And the idea is to shrink the balance from
where it is today, but, you know, we'll see

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:46.330
over the next few years, we can think hard
about should the balance sheet shrink all

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:52.700
the way down to where we had just maybe $25
billion of excess reserves, or we want to

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:55.679
take a different stance, and what's the right
amount of excess reserves?

00:23:55.679 --> 00:23:57.549
And this is an ongoing research topic.

00:23:57.549 --> 00:23:58.750
CLARIDA: Thank you.

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.830
One more from me, and then we'll turn it to
the audience.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:04.629
So be thinking of the questions you'd like
to ask.

00:24:04.629 --> 00:24:10.300
One interesting development in the U.S. labor
market is after several years in which labor

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:15.600
force participation was declining, it began
to pick up about a year, year and a half ago,

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.680
as some discouraged workers reentered the
labor force.

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:24.080
So, how do you and others think about that
as potentially a positive for the supply side

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:29.360
of the economy and for those individuals getting
back in the labor market?

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:31.940
So how much more potentially do we have to
go on that?

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:32.940
WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:33.940
Well, that's a big question.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:35.870
I mean, you know, we don't know.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:38.139
We're not that good at predicting labor force
participation.

00:24:38.139 --> 00:24:42.360
I think that we-during the recession, we saw
two big developments.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:47.960
One was dramatic decline in labor force participation
because of retirement of the baby boomers

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:48.960
and things like that.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:52.169
So that's kind of the part that we more or
less understand.

00:24:52.169 --> 00:24:55.700
The other was a decline that I think was due
to cyclical factors.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:25:00.909
It was the worst recession in, you know, 50
years, it lasted a long time, people did drop

00:25:00.909 --> 00:25:04.129
out of the labor force who actually do want
to have a job.

00:25:04.129 --> 00:25:08.830
So as we've seen the labor market get better
we've been adding, you know, millions of jobs

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:12.990
in the last several years, we are seeing some
people come back into the labor force, and

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:17.179
as we mentioned last year a noticeable pick
up there.

00:25:17.179 --> 00:25:22.590
So my own view is that most of the-you know,
where labor force participation is today,

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:23.850
most of that is structural.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:27.850
Most of that-the declines that we've seen
is due to demographics and things.

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:33.110
But we definitely could see some further increases
in participation as we pull more and more

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:38.260
people into the labor force who had basically
been sidelined during the recession and during

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:39.519
the recovery.

00:25:39.519 --> 00:25:40.519
It is a question mark.

00:25:40.519 --> 00:25:45.850
It's one of those things that, you know, we
have to continually update our estimates.

00:25:45.850 --> 00:25:49.960
One of the things-you know, when you're a
policymaker, you have to basically be (bazy

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:53.889
?). And you're always, you know, reassessing
your assumptions, you're reassessing your

00:25:53.889 --> 00:25:56.129
views based on the incoming data.

00:25:56.129 --> 00:26:00.220
And if labor force participation does pick
up-continue to pick up, then I, you know,

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:04.510
would probably be more optimistic about how
much further increase we could see.

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:10.299
Right now I feel like we're probably close
to the trend, but I have an open mind on that.

00:26:10.299 --> 00:26:11.299
So what's it mean?

00:26:11.299 --> 00:26:14.769
Well, what we've seen in the last several
months, even though we've been adding a lot

00:26:14.769 --> 00:26:17.720
of jobs, unemployment has not been coming
down.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.860
It means more supply side to the economy.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:22.100
And so it's a positive development.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:25.240
When I look at some of the statistics that
they collect, they actually ask a question

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:31.460
in the monthly survey of households, and they
do ask the question: If you're not in the

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:36.330
labor force-meaning you're neither working
nor actively searching for a job-do you want

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:37.330
to work?

00:26:37.330 --> 00:26:40.380
And that number of people who want to work
but aren't in the labor force has shrunken

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:41.889
dramatically over the last few years.

00:26:41.889 --> 00:26:46.110
So at least from that question, there doesn't
seem to be a lot of people still standing

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:47.110
on the sidelines.

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:48.110
CLARIDA: OK.

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:53.730
So at this point I'd like to invite members
to join in the conversation with their questions,

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:58.150
and reminding this meeting is on the record.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:00.530
So if you have a question, raise your hand.

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:04.020
A microphone will be brought to you.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:07.929
Please stand, speak directly into it, your
name and affiliation.

00:27:07.929 --> 00:27:12.820
And please limit yourself to one question
and keep it a question.

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:13.820
So let's see.

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:17.539
I see Willem Buiter there, so we'll let Willem
kick it off.

00:27:17.539 --> 00:27:22.029
WILLIAMS: Willem, the way to turn a statement
into a question is just to say, don't you

00:27:22.029 --> 00:27:24.200
agree, at the end?

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:25.200
(Laughter.)

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:26.200
Q: Right.

00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:28.730
Willem Buiter, Citi and Council on Foreign
Relations.

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:34.970
I was somewhat shocked by not surprised to
see-hear no reference to the words "financial

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:37.519
stability" in the entire exchange here.

00:27:37.519 --> 00:27:42.909
The Fed has not just a monetary policy dual
mandate-in fact, it's a triple mandate, (model

00:27:42.909 --> 00:27:47.360
?) long-term interest rates, but never mind-it
has a financial stability mandate.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:50.040
And much of the Federal Reserve Act is, in
fact, about this.

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:54.909
Now, we clearly are in the U.S. suffering
from late-cycle syndrome, right?

00:27:54.909 --> 00:27:57.610
Non-financial corporate credit, especially
high-yield.

00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:01.659
We see CLOs backed by subprime car loans in
large amounts.

00:28:01.659 --> 00:28:06.230
We even see, again, CLOs backed by residential
mortgages-never thought I'd see those again-subprime

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:07.499
residential mortgages.

00:28:07.499 --> 00:28:12.120
Now, the Fed has not countercyclical macroprudential
instruments.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:17.390
Is there a case for raising the policy rate
for financial stability reasons, even when

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:19.659
it's not warranted for dual-mandate reasons?

00:28:19.659 --> 00:28:21.879
Do you agree?

00:28:21.879 --> 00:28:22.879
(Laughter.)

00:28:22.879 --> 00:28:25.580
WILLIAMS: Don't you agree?

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:26.580
(Laughs.)

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:30.080
No, it's a great-it's a great question.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.190
And I agree completely with the premise.

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:38.200
The Federal Reserve was created 103 years
ago for financial stability reasons.

00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:43.679
And monetary policy as we're talking about
it is a relatively modern phenomenon.

00:28:43.679 --> 00:28:50.130
So the way I view the issue in terms of financial
stability is that, you know, given that we

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:55.440
are close to our dual mandate goals-essentially
at full employment and I believe on the inflation

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:01.190
side we're, you know, not only close but moving
towards it, I do take seriously the notion

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:08.730
that very low interest rates for a very long
period of time does create-as we know from

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:17.149
history and from the study of this topic-does
create a lot of basically reach for yield,

00:29:17.149 --> 00:29:25.389
risk-taking channel, and can contribute to
I think investors and, you know, I think other

00:29:25.389 --> 00:29:29.059
households and businesses taking on risks
that actually don't make sense in the long

00:29:29.059 --> 00:29:30.059
run.

00:29:30.059 --> 00:29:32.460
They only make sense in the environment of
very low interest rates.

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:37.090
And so one of the reasons we don't want to
keep interest rates excessively low and just

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:41.639
let's see where this economy takes us, if
you will, is because I believe these risks

00:29:41.639 --> 00:29:43.190
will grow over time.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:49.110
Right now, I don't think we're in a significant
risk of a-in terms of financial stability,

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:51.659
or risks of-systemic risks right now.

00:29:51.659 --> 00:29:54.549
But we don't want those to occur either.

00:29:54.549 --> 00:29:59.159
So I do agree that one of the arguments of
normalizing monetary policy, given where we

00:29:59.159 --> 00:30:06.340
are in our macro goals, is to make sure that
those financial stability risks don't further

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:08.460
increase and become more of an issue.

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:13.960
I don't think we face really a tradeoff right
now, because I think that the macro situation

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:19.179
supports raising interest rates, as well as
the financial stability issues that Willem

00:30:19.179 --> 00:30:20.179
raised.

00:30:20.179 --> 00:30:25.179
So I don't see this as a tension in terms
of monetary policy.

00:30:25.179 --> 00:30:28.929
In terms of macro-prudential tools, I agree
with the point-I made this point in a number

00:30:28.929 --> 00:30:33.650
of speeches-everyone loves to say our first
line of defense should be macro-prudential

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:34.650
tools.

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:39.259
Well, in the U.S., unlike some countries,
we don't really have explicitly macro-prudential

00:30:39.259 --> 00:30:40.259
tools.

00:30:40.259 --> 00:30:43.330
So what we need to do-macro-prudential tools
are things, if you think about it, in other

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:49.011
countries where the government will just say
the loan-to-value ratio on mortgages, they

00:30:49.011 --> 00:30:54.170
basically lower the level on what's allowed
on new mortgages from, say, you know, 80 percent

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:57.460
to 60 percent, that just is required throughout
the country.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:59.720
Well, we don't have those kind of tools.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:04.840
But we do have some tools that have a macro-prudential
kind of use.

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:10.460
And I specifically refer to the Fed's stress
tests, or CCAR process, where basically if

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:16.470
we're worried about significant risks in parts
of the financial system, we have, and we can

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:24.529
build those into our stress test to make sure
our largest banks can withstand a major decline

00:31:24.529 --> 00:31:29.080
in asset values or other events in those areas
of the economy.

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:35.259
So I think we do have some tools that we can
bring a macro-prudential perspective or framework

00:31:35.259 --> 00:31:36.259
to.

00:31:36.259 --> 00:31:41.139
But I do think this is something that-you
know, whenever I hear someone hear, well,

00:31:41.139 --> 00:31:42.499
macroprudential's our first line of defense.

00:31:42.499 --> 00:31:45.840
In the U.S. it's really micro-prudential,
which means strong regulation and supervision

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:50.080
of the financial institutions, plus some of
these other tools where you can bring more

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.169
of what we call macropru perspective.

00:31:52.169 --> 00:31:55.769
CLARIDA: Next question, please.

00:31:55.769 --> 00:31:58.659
Right there in the blue jacket, yes.

00:31:58.659 --> 00:32:03.340
Q: Rollie Flynn, Singa Consulting and Georgetown
University.

00:32:03.340 --> 00:32:07.929
We all heard and read about the New York Fed,
the cyber-hacking.

00:32:07.929 --> 00:32:13.320
Could you comment on what you and San Francisco
are doing to bolster your cyber defenses,

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:17.860
both technically and in terms of your management
structure?

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:19.360
WILLIAMS: Sure.

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:27.740
And you know, this issue of cybersecurity
is obviously incredibly important, not only

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:32.580
for, you know, for banks, for businesses,
for all of us, but at the Fed we take it very,

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:34.399
very seriously.

00:32:34.399 --> 00:32:40.419
The Fed does operate the largest payments
systems in the U.S. economy, whether it's

00:32:40.419 --> 00:32:43.110
Fedwire, whether it's check or ACH.

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:52.059
So we do enormous investment in terms of making
sure that our systems are safe and cannot-are

00:32:52.059 --> 00:32:54.159
bulletproof, as well as we can.

00:32:54.159 --> 00:32:59.509
I think a couple things that means is not
only investing in, you know, hardware and

00:32:59.509 --> 00:33:07.889
software and personnel, but also having pretty-very
stringent programs around employees, insider

00:33:07.889 --> 00:33:13.470
threats, thinking hard about, you know, what
all those risks are now how those are changing

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:15.080
all the time.

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:16.730
So this is a big area for us.

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:20.509
But it's also for our own bank and for our
own system.

00:33:20.509 --> 00:33:23.570
And obviously when we think of the Federal
Reserve System, there's 12 Federal Reserve

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:25.169
Banks, there's the Board of Governors.

00:33:25.169 --> 00:33:29.470
All of us are working together on all these
cybersecurity risks to the system, not individually.

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:32.480
And San Francisco doesn't have its own cybersecurity
program.

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.669
It's a Federal Reserve program.

00:33:34.669 --> 00:33:39.820
But then we're working hard with the banks
that we regulate and supervise to make sure

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:47.990
that they are putting in place stronger cybersecurity
defenses, being much more proactive, much

00:33:47.990 --> 00:33:52.520
more, you know, modern in their thinking and
their investments there.

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:57.379
So when a lot of people ask the question of,
you know, we're seeing a lot of-the smallest

00:33:57.379 --> 00:34:01.669
community banks either disappear or merge,
you know, why is that?

00:34:01.669 --> 00:34:05.210
You know, I actually think cybersecurity is
one of the biggest challenges for the smallest

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:10.280
institutions because it's just a fixed-it's
a fixed cost of running a business today.

00:34:10.280 --> 00:34:15.240
The last thing that I think we really need
to do is to see banks and financial institutions

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:19.700
definitely working better together about this,
because there's a competitive advantage if

00:34:19.700 --> 00:34:23.770
you can say we're better at cybersecurity
than my competitor you should do business

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:25.090
with us.

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:26.090
And that's true.

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:30.120
But we also want to make sure that we're sharing
information across institutions and working

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:31.730
together.

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:37.050
In terms of-you know, I think the biggest
challenge for us is this is a threat that

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:43.470
is becoming more and more sophisticated, with
a lot of money behind it, and obviously at

00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:45.250
times foreign governments.

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:51.510
So this is not, you know, the kid in Berkeley
living in his parents' garage anymore.

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:52.510
(Laughter.)

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:53.801
We are way, way beyond that.

00:34:53.801 --> 00:34:55.270
And we understand that.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:35:01.670
And in terms of our own policies we've instituted
at our own-at the Fed, but obviously at the

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:08.760
San Francisco Fed too-we instituted a number
of programs that basically restrict us significantly

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:15.230
in terms of, you know, background-we do background
checks, we do a lot more work on basically

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:21.800
making sure that access to the payment system,
to what we call the backbone of the Fed, is

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:22.800
very tightly controlled.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:31.160
And one of the key advantages of that, apparently,
is that I still use a BlackBerry.

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.160
(Laughter.)

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.760
CLARIDA: Right there in the front table.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:39.160
Q: Good morning.

00:35:39.160 --> 00:35:40.320
My name is Nili Gilbert.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:42.780
I'm a co-founder of Matarian Capital.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:43.780
Thank you.

00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:47.140
Thanks to you both for being with us here
today.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:53.620
Recently at a speech in Sacramento you contrasted
the productivity gains that saw after the

00:35:53.620 --> 00:36:00.450
tech boom in the '90s with the lack of productivity
gains that we've seen in the context of current

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:02.920
technological innovation.

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:07.480
And you observed that perhaps this is because
some of the current innovations are more focused

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:13.640
on how we spend our leisure time, whereas
the prior tech boom affected businesses more.

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:19.860
I would observe that some of the current innovations
are also affecting our labor markets, in the

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:25.110
context of companies like Uber, Airbnb, TaskRabbit.

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:31.450
I was wondering if you would be willing to
speak further about how you see innovation

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:38.090
affecting the U.S. economy at this time, and
in particular from your perch in San Francisco,

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:42.730
right next door to Silicon Valley where many
of these companies are housed and many of

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:44.730
these technologies are being used.

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:50.980
Is there anything to be observed in the regional
data that can help us to understand how innovative

00:36:50.980 --> 00:36:53.040
technologies are affecting the economy?

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:54.040
WILLIAMS: Sure.

00:36:54.040 --> 00:37:00.220
That's a great set of questions that we focus
a lot on in the San Francisco Fed.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:05.410
So one of the things I said in the speech,
as you correctly pointed out, is that productivity

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:08.900
growth in the U.S. economy is actually back
to its old normal.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:12.260
This is something that Bob Gordon has written
about in his book and many economists have

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:15.820
studied, including one of my colleagues at
the San Francisco Fed, John Fernald.

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:19.250
And basically, normal productivity growth
in the U.S. is about 1 1/2 percent.

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:23.510
And then we get-during our history we get-in
the U.S. and other countries there's these

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:30.050
periods of surges in productivity growth often
associated with a whole set of fundamental

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:35.720
breakthroughs in technology, whether it's
electrification, whether it's internal combustion

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:36.930
engine, or things like that.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:41.520
And they all come together, and they basically
transform what we make in our economy and

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:42.660
how we make it.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:49.600
And we had a mini-boom in the '90s around
high-tech, especially around basically computers,

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:52.280
the Internet, and all of that, and it lasted
for about 10 years.

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:56.770
It was the smallest productivity boom-a small
productivity boom compared to the others.

00:37:56.770 --> 00:38:00.470
And the point I made about this one is we're
not seeing it in the productivity data.

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:04.670
So if you live in the-you know, by the way,
when you live in-if you're in San Francisco,

00:38:04.670 --> 00:38:08.720
you're not in Silicon Valley, but everybody
who works in Silicon Valley lives in San Francisco

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:09.720
now.

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:11.990
So you-basically, they're creating all the
traffic and everything.

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:12.990
(Laughter.)

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:14.980
So you feel like you're in Silicon Valley.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:18.260
So what we're seeing is a couple things.

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:21.890
I think-you know, why we're not seeing a big
productivity boom?

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:26.230
One is, is that some of it's just-is really
that it's about leisure time.

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:30.410
It's about sitting on your phone if you're
on the train or the bus or, you know, at home,

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:35.840
and you're, you know, seeing all the amazing
things that cats can do which you never knew

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:36.840
before.

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:37.840
(Laughter.)

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:38.840
OK, so that's great.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:39.840
I'm not against it.

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:40.840
I love cats.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:41.840
I say this and people say, you don't like
cats.

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:42.840
No, love cats.

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:43.840
But that's not really GDP.

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:44.990
It doesn't show up as GDP.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:45.990
(Laughter.)

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:50.390
And in fact, it could be negative for productivity
when you're doing it in your workplace.

00:38:50.390 --> 00:38:51.440
(Laughter.)

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:53.690
So that doesn't show up in GDP.

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:55.070
Maybe it shows up in utility.

00:38:55.070 --> 00:38:58.930
Maybe it makes us happier and stuff, but happiness
isn't what we're measuring when we measure

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:01.950
output per worker in the business sector.

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:08.570
The other thing is we don't produce computers
in the U.S. very much anymore.

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:10.950
Back in the '90s, we were actually building
the computers.

00:39:10.950 --> 00:39:14.840
The chips were built-you know, made by Intel
or other companies.

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:16.920
Computers were often assembled here.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:21.580
But a lot of this was actually investment
in the United States, in very high productivity

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.720
jobs in the United States in the hardware
industry.

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:29.650
Well, the hardware industry has mostly moved
out of the U.S., has moved to Asia, and now

00:39:29.650 --> 00:39:32.620
a lot of the innovation is really in software.

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:37.610
And that just doesn't affect productivity
in the same way that all of those jobs-factory

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:40.140
jobs that we saw in the '90s.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:46.200
Now, in terms of-you know, if you're in Silicon
Valley they say, no, John, you don't understand;

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:47.200
we're mismeasuring.

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:48.310
It's the accountants that are wrong.

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:50.140
It's the-you know, it's the economists.

00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:53.250
It's the government, you know, statisticians.

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:59.170
But again, my colleague John Fernald just
did a really good Brookings paper in-a couple

00:39:59.170 --> 00:40:00.170
of papers now on this.

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:04.030
And, you know, if you look at the productivity
slowdown, no matter how you slice and dice

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:09.390
it, or how you try to, you know, get all the
gig economy and Google and Facebook and all

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:14.240
these things measured as best as you can,
it still does-it doesn't really change the

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:15.240
story.

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:17.620
The productivity slowdown is real.

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:22.820
Whatever we've seen in the last 10 years is
likely to be what we'll see in the next 10

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:23.820
years.

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:29.770
And it's just basically because the newest
technologies haven't fundamentally changed

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:34.810
what we produce and how we produce it in the
U.S. in a way that earlier ones have.

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:38.350
Now, going back to the gig economy, I'm going
to say something else that people in Silicon

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:42.150
Valley just shake their heads and say, you're
an economist, you don't understand.

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:43.480
It's still tiny.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:45.440
Even in the Bay Area, it's tiny.

00:40:45.440 --> 00:40:50.540
You know, companies like Uber and Lyft and
those, I mean, the tax industry is basically

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:54.670
a rounding error to the U.S. GDP; Airbnb,
these things.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.870
I think they're important, and I think they
have important issues about what employees

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:03.430
are, issues about full-time/part-time employment
and things like that, a lot of issues that

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:10.800
I think our legal system or kind of regulations
need to evolve to fully incorporate.

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:14.360
But in terms of the economy, it's still a
pretty small part of the economy.

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:16.220
So what are-what is the U.S. economy today?

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:22.810
I think a lot of people, you know, I think
are thinking about kind where we are in 2016.

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:27.340
It's not only a service-sector economy, it
is a primarily service economy.

00:41:27.340 --> 00:41:28.780
So what are services?

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:32.260
If you look at the national income accounts,
it's health care, it's education.

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:36.400
It's also housing, but you know, so it's just
abstract from that.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:40.131
But where the U.S. economy, you know, creates
the jobs?

00:41:40.131 --> 00:41:43.970
Over 90 percent of the jobs created in the
U.S. are in services last year.

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:45.630
Where is our GDP growth?

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:47.500
It's in services.

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:49.140
Manufacturing is a very small part of our
employment.

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:52.630
It's still a part of the GDP.

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:56.340
And so when you think about-whenever you think
about these changes that are happening, you

00:41:56.340 --> 00:42:00.800
just got to go back to say how does this fundamentally
change the delivery of health care?

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:05.940
How does it fundamentally change education?

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:09.750
And how does it fundamentally change what
we produce in our economy?

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:11.260
So these are great open questions.

00:42:11.260 --> 00:42:15.660
But right now, you know, we're studying them
actively.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:19.870
I think there are a lot of regulatory/legal
issues that need to be thought about.

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:23.821
But in terms of the macroeconomy, it's just
not having the impact that I think a lot of

00:42:23.821 --> 00:42:26.100
people would have expected.

00:42:26.100 --> 00:42:31.350
By the way, even if you try to measure this
utility thing, some people have studied how

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:37.440
many hours people spend on their iPhones and,
you know, Android phones, and for me on my

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:41.320
BlackBerry-but of course, I can't do anything
cool on my BlackBerry-(laughter)-what we're

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:46.010
doing is we're basically not watching-well,
I should say it right-we're watching TV on

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:47.350
our phones now.

00:42:47.350 --> 00:42:52.480
So it's basically a movement of advertising
revenue and eyeballs from newspapers, magazines,

00:42:52.480 --> 00:42:58.070
and TV-you know, old TV, you know, the boxes
that you watch in your living room-to these

00:42:58.070 --> 00:42:59.070
phones.

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:05.640
So it doesn't-again, it's cool, it's interesting,
people love it, but it doesn't create a lot

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:12.250
of GDP and productivity because it's basically
just moving one activity from one device to

00:43:12.250 --> 00:43:13.250
another.

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:16.160
And I think I probably out-geeked what I needed
to do on that question.

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:17.160
(Laughter.)

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:19.450
CLARIDA: Right there at the front right table,
yeah.

00:43:19.450 --> 00:43:21.850
Q: Thank you.

00:43:21.850 --> 00:43:22.960
Stephen Blank.

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:28.440
The views-the rather rosy scenario you described
about our economy couldn't be more different

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:33.770
than the views on the economy expressed by
our emerging political leaders and agreed

00:43:33.770 --> 00:43:36.030
on with many of their followers.

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:38.510
What do you think explains this variance?

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:42.940
And, more important, what does it portend
for the relationship between the Fed and the

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:46.480
emerging political leadership we'll see in
the next-in the next years?

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:47.771
WILLIAMS: It's a great question.

00:43:47.771 --> 00:43:53.882
And I think it's actually pretty easy to understand,
because at the Fed we're interested in our

00:43:53.882 --> 00:43:57.970
dual-mandate goals, again, which is 2 percent
inflation and what we think of as maximum

00:43:57.970 --> 00:43:59.320
employment.

00:43:59.320 --> 00:44:05.440
And we just take the supply side of the economy,
the structure of the economy, fiscal policy,

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:06.980
we just take that as given.

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:11.470
This is the economy that we're handed, and
how do we best achieve our goal?

00:44:11.470 --> 00:44:17.620
So the way I view it is-the Fed, in my perspective-we've
done what we can in order to get the economy,

00:44:17.620 --> 00:44:21.000
you know, basically back to full strength
with interest rates.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:27.350
Interest rates can only do so much, and obviously
they can't solve structural or other problems.

00:44:27.350 --> 00:44:30.010
We're doing what we can to get inflation back
to 2 percent.

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:34.260
So what are the issues that really are the
big economic issues in the U.S. economy?

00:44:34.260 --> 00:44:39.310
They are much longer-bigger, longer-term issues
about the supply side, if you will, of the

00:44:39.310 --> 00:44:43.640
economy: the fact that productivity growth
is only 1 1/2 percent; the fact that trend

00:44:43.640 --> 00:44:48.340
GDP growth is 2 percent or even a bit less
than that, which is the lowest we have experienced

00:44:48.340 --> 00:44:53.580
in our lifetimes; the fact that, you know,
income inequality is rising, or however you

00:44:53.580 --> 00:44:56.970
think about these-or economic mobility seems
to be declining somewhat.

00:44:56.970 --> 00:45:01.090
All these issues-the hollowing out of the
middle class-these are all real.

00:45:01.090 --> 00:45:02.280
These have been studied.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:06.910
They have to do with a lot of different dynamics
over the last several decades.

00:45:06.910 --> 00:45:10.480
But they're not the things that monetary policy
can or should address.

00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:14.130
And so when I sound optimistic-and I hear-you
know, it's a great question, and I hear it

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:18.720
all the time-it's optimistic within the confines
of what can monetary policy do.

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:22.590
Monetary policy can get unemployment down
from 10 percent, which was our peak, down

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:25.670
to 9 (percent), to 8 (percent), to 5 (percent),
or maybe a little bit lower.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:30.130
But if we try to do more than that, we just
create other problems, whether it's the financial

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:34.880
stability issues that Willem highlighted,
or just creating inflation or other problems.

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:37.580
So I think that this is the disconnect.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:42.260
I'm optimistic within the context of the hand
that we're dealt with-dealt.

00:45:42.260 --> 00:45:46.670
I think the concerns a lot of people raise
is that the hand isn't one that's favorable.

00:45:46.670 --> 00:45:51.800
I mean, trend growth is lower than any time
we've seen before, all these other structural

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:52.800
issues.

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:55.760
And again, I can't do anything about that.

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:58.020
So you asked, what's it mean for the Fed?

00:45:58.020 --> 00:46:04.180
I think it does, I think, you know, create
a lot of these-this tension about, you know,

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:08.240
the last several years people have been saying
the Fed's the only game in town, right, you

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:09.240
know?

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:13.880
So they're saying, you know, fiscal policy
at times has been at loggerheads, other policies,

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:18.640
you know, or other issues-you know, we've
had government shutdowns and things like that-so

00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:21.170
we're counting on the Fed to solve our problems.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:28.040
And how I'm hearing more and more of these
kind of, you know, claims or, you know, people

00:46:28.040 --> 00:46:30.430
raising issues: well, can't the Fed solve
this problem?

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:33.980
I mean, you guys were amazing, you know, low
interest rates, big balance sheet.

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:38.980
You got $4 trillion; can't you use that to
deal with these structural problems?

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:42.090
And we know from history, both in the U.S.
and in other countries, that when you try

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:47.460
to solve structural problems, longer-term
problems, with monetary policy, it leads-you

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:49.550
know, it never ends well.

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:52.660
And I think that that is going to be something
we're going to probably see more pressure

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:58.780
on us to try to use monetary policy to solve
longer-run structural problems, and we just

00:46:58.780 --> 00:47:01.190
have to fight that every day.

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:03.860
CLARIDA: Right there.

00:47:03.860 --> 00:47:06.850
Mmm hmm, next to Willem.

00:47:06.850 --> 00:47:10.270
Q: Mario Platero, Council on Foreign Relations
and Sole 24 Ore.

00:47:10.270 --> 00:47:13.700
I just wanted to go back to the short term.

00:47:13.700 --> 00:47:21.770
And we have seen some retailers that have
done pretty badly recently, and we have seen

00:47:21.770 --> 00:47:24.330
housing in New York that is slowing down.

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:30.460
Apparently, there is not such a great sale
of the new condos that have been built up.

00:47:30.460 --> 00:47:35.060
And so the economy seems to be-you know, there
are some issues.

00:47:35.060 --> 00:47:41.170
So the question is, how do you put those items
into perspective?

00:47:41.170 --> 00:47:49.060
And what kind of bias the slowing of the economy
would have in your, you know, monetary policy

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:52.650
decision-making in the short/medium term?

00:47:52.650 --> 00:47:56.430
We know Dudley last week said that, you know,
maybe by June there could be an increase of

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:57.430
rate.

00:47:57.430 --> 00:48:02.510
So would the economy intervene, or that is
pretty much a decided factor?

00:48:02.510 --> 00:48:03.510
Thank you.

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:07.090
WILLIAMS: So this is-you know, when we say
we're data dependent-and I always say, you

00:48:07.090 --> 00:48:11.790
know, monetary policy is data dependent-it
goes right to your question, right?

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:17.840
I mean, wait a minute, what data are you looking
at, which data are decisive in terms of decisions.

00:48:17.840 --> 00:48:22.740
And of course, when we say data, there's millions
of data points that we observe and collect

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:23.740
and analyze.

00:48:23.740 --> 00:48:28.610
But I, again, go back to what's it-you know,
what's it mean for our goals of maximum employment

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:31.500
and 2 percent inflation, or price stability?

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:34.060
So I look at the data, again, from a two-year
perspective.

00:48:34.060 --> 00:48:37.890
I agree with your point that we're seeing
some softening in some parts of the hottest

00:48:37.890 --> 00:48:42.320
commercial real estate in the-in the-you know,
the globe, New York and San Francisco.

00:48:42.320 --> 00:48:47.860
But remember, this is in the context of, like,
all of my employees would love to see some

00:48:47.860 --> 00:48:49.600
declines in rents in San Francisco.

00:48:49.600 --> 00:48:53.210
So, you know, it's in the context of a very
hot market.

00:48:53.210 --> 00:48:56.530
So we have to read through that data, try
to analyze it best we can.

00:48:56.530 --> 00:49:01.720
That Q1 GDP growth, which was very soft, you
know, to what extent does that portend softness

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:02.720
in second quarter?

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:07.140
So far, I think the data are showing that
that was an aberration and anomalous, and

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:08.670
the second quarter's looking better.

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:10.050
But we do want to keep watching that.

00:49:10.050 --> 00:49:14.470
We'll get employment data for-you know, for
this month, and we'll get more-you know, more

00:49:14.470 --> 00:49:15.690
data on the economy.

00:49:15.690 --> 00:49:19.760
We have to take all of it together and try
to get this big picture of what's it tell

00:49:19.760 --> 00:49:21.680
us where the economy is going over the next
year or two.

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:26.820
In terms of the retail sales, this is one
of the reasons macroeconomics is not a stagnant

00:49:26.820 --> 00:49:29.650
kind of subject or field.

00:49:29.650 --> 00:49:34.870
A lot of what we're seeing is this mix between
bricks and mortar, whether you think about

00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:38.980
the big department stores like Nordstrom or
Macy's or others, versus online sales.

00:49:38.980 --> 00:49:42.940
So we-you know, we have to make sure the-you
know, the data we're looking at is not just

00:49:42.940 --> 00:49:47.300
what's happening in one part of retail, but
it capturing the broader trends.

00:49:47.300 --> 00:49:52.810
And of course, our statistics have done a-have
to do a good job of keeping up with that.

00:49:52.810 --> 00:49:56.390
In terms of where we are in terms of monetary
policy, since you did ask, it's the first

00:49:56.390 --> 00:50:01.090
time I've heard the word "June" mentioned
this morning, so, OK, I'll go-I'll bite.

00:50:01.090 --> 00:50:03.220
You know, it is data dependent.

00:50:03.220 --> 00:50:07.830
And what that means is, you know, Rich asked
me, you know, what kind of rate increases

00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:09.770
over the rest of the year.

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:14.470
That's going to-you know, if the data come
in softer than expected, you know, all else

00:50:14.470 --> 00:50:18.940
equal, that tells you we'll move a little
bit slower or maybe a little bit later in

00:50:18.940 --> 00:50:20.420
terms of the next rate increase.

00:50:20.420 --> 00:50:25.910
But, you know, my own view is that, you know,
the data stream we've been getting over the

00:50:25.910 --> 00:50:29.900
last several months has been-you know, I always
compare it to what I was forecasting a few

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:32.090
months ago or a few quarters ago.

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:36.040
And my view is, in terms of employment and
inflation, the data-the data we've been getting

00:50:36.040 --> 00:50:41.030
in the last few months or quarters is basically
consistent with the forecast I've had.

00:50:41.030 --> 00:50:44.590
So, you know, there are some ups and some
downs, you know, and everyone gets caught

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:49.461
up in that labor report or that retail sales
or that, you know, manufacturing report, but

00:50:49.461 --> 00:50:54.130
overall I view that the data have been consistent
with, you know, a good 2 percent growth and

00:50:54.130 --> 00:50:56.390
steady job gains.

00:50:56.390 --> 00:50:57.720
CLARIDA: Right there.

00:50:57.720 --> 00:50:58.880
Straight on.

00:50:58.880 --> 00:50:59.880
Yes.

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:01.950
Q: My name is Peter Goldmark.

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:07.210
I'm an independent consultant to foundations
in the climate and energy area.

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:12.700
This is a room where a lot of people will
be familiar with Mervyn King's "End of Alchemy."

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:17.270
And I wondered if you would share with us
your reaction to his recommendations to, quote,

00:51:17.270 --> 00:51:23.020
"avoid the next financial crisis"-the two-track
system, wide banking, narrow banking, different

00:51:23.020 --> 00:51:24.140
reserve policies.

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:25.400
What are your thoughts on that?

00:51:25.400 --> 00:51:26.880
I found it very powerful.

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:33.170
WILLIAMS: So, you know, there's-obviously
there was a lot of reassessment/reevaluation

00:51:33.170 --> 00:51:39.910
of the appropriate regulatory framework after
the financial crisis-a lot of studies being

00:51:39.910 --> 00:51:44.230
done by universities, by academics, and policymakers.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:52.080
In the U.S., I think that the approach that
has been taken so far has hit the most important

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:57.930
kind of risks to the financial system in terms
of making sure that our biggest banks have

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:02.030
adequate capital liquidity, that we put in
enhanced supervision.

00:52:02.030 --> 00:52:05.490
The bigger you are, the more supervision and
regulation you get, and the more demands we

00:52:05.490 --> 00:52:10.630
have for you for capital liquidity-basically
putting on, if you will, as an economist,

00:52:10.630 --> 00:52:14.950
like, a-if you create a negative externality,
meaning that by being very large you create

00:52:14.950 --> 00:52:23.920
a risk of too big to fail, that we put more-basically,
more requirements on you, making that a-making

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:29.530
you make the tradeoff between should we be
big and take on all the extra regulatory requirements,

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:32.750
supervisory requirements, or choose not to
be big.

00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:38.770
So I think that the approach we've taken so
far has been the right one in general.

00:52:38.770 --> 00:52:39.840
It's very complicated.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:41.310
It's got-you know, nothing's perfect.

00:52:41.310 --> 00:52:46.670
But right now I feel that the-you know, in
terms of the largest banks, in terms of the,

00:52:46.670 --> 00:52:51.641
you know, financial system broadly, we're
in a much better place than we were 10 years

00:52:51.641 --> 00:52:52.641
ago.

00:52:52.641 --> 00:52:57.000
However, I think that the issue that we should
be kind of reassessing or reevaluating over

00:52:57.000 --> 00:53:00.650
the next five to 10 years, or thinking about
in terms of the future, the way-getting to

00:53:00.650 --> 00:53:06.180
your question, is, what do we do in the U.S.
around the shadow banking system?

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:12.580
Which is now-because we're-gotten more regulation,
more requirements on the regulated depository

00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:17.040
institutions, it's just obviously pushed more
and more activity to the non-depository institutions,

00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:18.600
or the shadow banking.

00:53:18.600 --> 00:53:22.770
And I think that this is the area where I-where
I worry.

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:26.900
As it's grown, it creates a lot of systemic
risk.

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:33.460
Obviously, we saw that in the financial crisis
with, you know, AIG or Bear Stearns or Lehman

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:36.150
or, you know, money market mutual funds.

00:53:36.150 --> 00:53:41.100
So, to me, that's the question that we need
to be focused on, is how do we bring a regulatory

00:53:41.100 --> 00:53:47.010
framework that isn't focused-isn't designed
based on what your charter is, but basically

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:51.120
is designed more around how do we protect
the financial system as a whole.

00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:55.300
So when I look at what Britain has done, for
example, I mean, I think there are advantages

00:53:55.300 --> 00:54:00.420
to their approach, and I think there are advantages
of thinking about how do we create, you know,

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:05.540
a system that makes sure that we just haven't
basically protected one-created a Maginot

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:11.210
Line around the banks, but then created an
opportunity and actually pushed more activity

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:13.650
to areas that create more risk.

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:17.640
Right now, I have to admit that, you know,
the Fed, we're just focused on getting the

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:23.170
Dodd-Frank, if you will, regime in place,
getting the last few pieces, which has to

00:54:23.170 --> 00:54:28.390
do with living wills and all of that to the
place where, obviously, the regulatory-the

00:54:28.390 --> 00:54:30.900
Board of Governors, the FDIC are comfortable
with that.

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:35.260
I think, you know, once we have all of that,
then some of these kind of deeper, longer-term

00:54:35.260 --> 00:54:37.580
questions are ones we should be thinking hard
about.

00:54:37.580 --> 00:54:42.050
CLARIDA: And the last question will be right
there at the front table.

00:54:42.050 --> 00:54:43.790
Q: Good morning.

00:54:43.790 --> 00:54:45.720
Bhakti Mirchandani, One Williams Street.

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:47.190
Thanks for a great discussion.

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:53.420
In today's discussion, you mentioned two developments
that may limit the use of tried-and-true monetary

00:54:53.420 --> 00:54:54.620
policies in future crises.

00:54:54.620 --> 00:55:00.100
One is the change in equilibrium interest
rates to zero percent, and the other one is

00:55:00.100 --> 00:55:04.630
the quintupling of the Fed's balance sheet
since 2007.

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:09.500
Given both of these developments, what types
of unconventional monetary policies do you

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:12.540
expect the Fed to consider in the next crisis?

00:55:12.540 --> 00:55:18.370
WILLIAMS: Not just crisis, I think just even
in the next recession, I mean, or next situation.

00:55:18.370 --> 00:55:21.070
So let me-let me broaden that out a little
bit.

00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:27.080
So say that we had a-you know, it was called
a rosy scenario, but say that we get-for whatever

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:32.140
reason, my forecast of 2 percent growth, continuing
improvement in inflation doesn't come true.

00:55:32.140 --> 00:55:37.940
What tools do we have today to boost the economy,
to get us back on track?

00:55:37.940 --> 00:55:44.300
So I think it's the same toolkit we used from
2009 till today: basically, interest rates

00:55:44.300 --> 00:55:45.300
close to zero.

00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:50.140
You know, we could lower interest rates back
close to zero or not raise interest rates.

00:55:50.140 --> 00:55:53.680
That would be one way to stimulate the economy,
if it was needed.

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:55.450
The other is we could go back to QE.

00:55:55.450 --> 00:55:58.980
I don't think we're out of-at all out of ammo
on that.

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:03.810
I personally think that our asset-purchase
programs were successful in lowering long-term

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:08.880
interest rates, increasing-basically improving
financial conditions and boosting the economy.

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:10.110
So we can go back to that.

00:56:10.110 --> 00:56:15.600
I also feel the forward guidance that we used
in 2011 to 2012, basically saying we're not

00:56:15.600 --> 00:56:19.920
raising rates for the next couple of years,
was a very powerful tool to clarify what the

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:22.680
Fed's thinking was in that kind of circumstance.

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:26.590
We're not in that circumstance now, but at
the time it was-these were powerful tools.

00:56:26.590 --> 00:56:29.660
So I think that those are the same toolkit
we can go back to.

00:56:29.660 --> 00:56:30.680
We understand them better.

00:56:30.680 --> 00:56:33.690
I think we have more confidence in which are
the ones that work better under different

00:56:33.690 --> 00:56:37.040
circumstances, and we can go back to those
if those were necessary.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:38.770
You didn't ask, but I-you know, we're out
of time.

00:56:38.770 --> 00:56:43.161
I will say I don't think negative interest
rates is a tool that we would use, except

00:56:43.161 --> 00:56:45.260
at the-it would be at the bottom of that list.

00:56:45.260 --> 00:56:49.150
I think the tools that we would use in the
future would be very similar to the ones we've

00:56:49.150 --> 00:56:50.530
used in the past.

00:56:50.530 --> 00:56:54.290
What I do worry about is, when I look around
the globe, it's not the U.S. economy.

00:56:54.290 --> 00:56:55.710
I think the U.S. economy's in good shape.

00:56:55.710 --> 00:56:57.910
I think we're in a good place.

00:56:57.910 --> 00:57:03.430
You look at Japan, you look at Europe, you
look at ECB, Switzerland, Scandinavia, I mean,

00:57:03.430 --> 00:57:07.430
all of these countries are facing persistently
low inflation.

00:57:07.430 --> 00:57:11.441
It's going to take-you know, they've got-not
only do they have massive QE and they've got

00:57:11.441 --> 00:57:15.550
negative interest rates, very negative interest
rates in a few countries, and they're struggling

00:57:15.550 --> 00:57:17.640
to get inflation back on track.

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:21.050
So that's where-if you ask me what keeps me
up about thinking about monetary policy, it's

00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:25.170
really not the U.S. so much, but thinking
about what's happening there and learning

00:57:25.170 --> 00:57:29.620
from what they're doing in terms of the effectiveness
of the tools that they're deploying.

00:57:29.620 --> 00:57:30.620
CLARIDA: All right.

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:33.110
And on that note, we will conclude, right
on time.

00:57:33.110 --> 00:57:35.052
So thank you all, and have an enjoyable rest
of your day.

00:57:35.052 --> 00:57:36.052
Thank you.

00:57:36.052 --> 00:57:37.052
(Applause.)

00:57:37.052 --> 00:57:37.053
(END)

